{
  "Logging": {
    "LogLevel": {
      "Microsoft.AspNetCore": "Information",
      "PowerFramework.Security": "Debug"
    }
  },

  // ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  // KESTREL - THE SAME ENDPOINT KEY AS THE BASE FILE, OVERRIDDEN AND NOT ADDED TO
  // ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  //
  // THE TLS LISTENER IS UNCHANGED AND IS RESTATED RATHER THAN INHERITED. It is spelled out in full
  // because a reader of THIS file has to be able to see that the trust bootstrap is still TLS in
  // development.
  //
  // THE ENDPOINT NAME MUST BE `Default`, AND MATCHING IT IS THE WHOLE POINT OF RESTATING THE SECTION.
  // `Kestrel:Endpoints` is a DICTIONARY KEYED BY ENDPOINT NAME, and configuration merges per key - so a
  // differently named entry here does not replace the inherited one, it ADDS A SECOND LISTENER beside it.
  // This file previously named it `Https`, which left development binding two endpoints on the one
  // `https://+:5104` address and contradicted the base file's own statement that this service binds
  // exactly one endpoint in every environment. Reusing the name is what makes this an override.
  // Its certificate still comes from Kestrel__Certificates__Default__Path and __KeyPath in the
  // environment - for a local bring-up, a self-signed PEM pair generated by the operator. No path,
  // no password and no thumbprint appears in this file either (C-F).
  //
  // WHAT THIS OVERLAY ACTUALLY CHANGES: nothing about the endpoint at all. The whole section is restated
  // verbatim, and it is restated for the reason above rather than to alter anything - a reader of THIS
  // file has to be able to see the effective listener without holding two files in their head, and the
  // dictionary-merge behaviour means a differently named entry would ADD a second listener instead of
  // overriding this one. What the overlay changes is below the Kestrel section: the issuer claim and the
  // end-to-end suite's identity.
  //
  // THE SCHEME IS https IN EVERY ENVIRONMENT, INCLUDING THIS ONE, AND THE SUPERSEDED ARGUMENT FOR
  // PLAINTEXT IS RECORDED HERE SO IT IS NOT MADE AGAIN. That argument was: the attached environment's
  // readiness gate for this service is `curl -sf http://localhost:5104/health`, it supplies no
  // certificate material, and AAP 0.8.3 permits five deviations from that frozen contract - none of them
  // a transport change. Three things break at a plaintext listener, and none of them fails loudly
  // (CWE-319):
  //
  //   * TOKEN ISSUANCE BECOMES UNCALLABLE RATHER THAN WEAKLY AUTHENTICATED. `POST /v1/tokens` derives
  //     the caller's identity from the COMMON NAME of a presented client certificate, and a client
  //     certificate cannot exist on a plaintext listener at all - there is no handshake to present one
  //     inside. `ClientCertificateMode: AllowCertificate` below is meaningless without TLS.
  //   * A MINTED BEARER TOKEN WOULD CROSS THE WIRE IN CLEARTEXT, from the one service that holds the
  //     system's signing input, on the one request whose entire purpose is to hand out a credential.
  //   * AND THE ISSUER CLAIM BELOW IS WHERE A CONSUMER'S STOCK BEARER HANDLER DERIVES ITS METADATA
  //     ADDRESS FROM. A plaintext issuer sends all three verifiers to fetch the verification key set
  //     over a channel an attacker can rewrite, which is the one fetch in the estate that must not be
  //     rewritable - and it is why the three consuming services keep `RequireHttpsMetadata` true.
  //
  // The development relaxation this system permits is therefore a SELF-SIGNED certificate, never
  // plaintext: the certificate comes from Kestrel__Certificates__Default__Path and __KeyPath in the
  // environment, and the trust anchor the three verifiers build the chain against comes from
  // INTERNAL_TLS_TRUSTED_CA_PATH. No path, no password and no thumbprint appears in this file (C-F).
  // A readiness probe therefore uses `curl -sf --cacert <that anchor> https://localhost:5104/health`;
  // `-k` is not an acceptable substitute, because a probe that skips verification proves only that
  // something answered.
  "Kestrel": {
    "Endpoints": {
      "Default": {
        "Url": "https://+:5104",
        "Protocols": "Http1",
        "ClientCertificateMode": "AllowCertificate"
      }
    }
  },

  // The issuer claim follows the listener. It is the https loopback address in development for the
  // same reason the listener is TLS: a consumer's stock bearer handler derives the metadata address
  // from this value, and a plaintext issuer would send it to fetch the key set over a channel an
  // attacker can rewrite. That is why the scheme below is not relaxed for a local run - the
  // development relaxation this system permits is a self-signed certificate, never plaintext metadata.
  // The RequireHttpsMetadata setting that enforces this lives on the three CONSUMING services, which
  // perform the fetch; Security itself configures no Authority and no MetadataAddress and fetches no
  // metadata at all, so it carries no such setting - see the note in appsettings.json.
  "Security": {
    "Issuer": "https://localhost:5104",

    // ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
    // THE OPERATOR / END-TO-END IDENTITY, DECLARED HERE AND DELIBERATELY NOT IN THE BASE FILE
    // ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
    //
    // A CONFIGURATION ARRAY DOES NOT MERGE ELEMENT-WISE - IT MERGES BY INDEX - so this block REPLACES
    // element 0 of the base roster and appends nothing. That is why the two base entries are restated
    // below rather than assumed: an overlay declaring one element would leave the gateway entry
    // overwritten and the dataservices entry intact, which is the most confusing possible outcome and
    // exactly the shape of defect the sibling Kestrel comment in this file records for section keys.
    // Any change to the base roster must be mirrored here in the same commit.
    //
    // WHY THE SUITE IDENTITY IS A LOCAL FACT. `tests/e2e` presents SECURITY_CLIENT_ID and
    // SECURITY_CLIENT_SECRET as an HTTP Basic credential at the issuance edge, and its default subject
    // is `pfw-e2e-suite` [tests/e2e/fixtures/auth.ts:L212]. An operator running the documented
    // bring-up sets SECURITY_CLIENT_ID to exactly that value, because the issuance edge reconciles the
    // claimed subject against the authenticated credential identity ordinally - a mismatch is a 403,
    // by design. A production roster has no reason to carry a caller only a test presents, so it does
    // not.
    //
    // ITS GRANTS ARE THE SUITE'S OWN DECLARED MINIMUM and nothing more: `ping`, `capabilities` and
    // `datawindow` [tests/e2e/fixtures/auth.ts:L256-L260], across the four audiences the suite probes.
    // No wildcard and no administrative scope, which is the suite's own stated reason too - an identity
    // that asked for everything would stop being able to demonstrate that least privilege is enforced.
    "Clients": [
      {
        "Subject": "powerframework-gateway",
        "SecretConfigurationKey": "SECURITY_CLIENT_SECRET_GATEWAY",
        "Audiences": [
          "powerframework-dataservices",
          "powerframework-security"
        ],
        "Scopes": [
          "dataservices.datawindow",
          "dataservices.columnexpression",
          "ping"
        ]
      },
      {
        "Subject": "powerframework-dataservices",
        "SecretConfigurationKey": "SECURITY_CLIENT_SECRET_DATASERVICES",
        "Audiences": [
          "powerframework-persistence",
          "powerframework-security"
        ],
        "Scopes": [
          "persistence.read",
          "persistence.write",
          "security.crypto",
          "ping"
        ]
      },
      {
        "Subject": "pfw-e2e-suite",
        "SecretConfigurationKey": "SECURITY_CLIENT_SECRET",
        "Audiences": [
          "powerframework-gateway",
          "powerframework-dataservices",
          "powerframework-persistence",
          "powerframework-security"
        ],
        "Scopes": [
          "ping",
          "capabilities",
          "datawindow"
        ]
      }
    ],

    // ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
    // THE SUITE IDENTITY'S PERMISSION GRANT, IN THE FLAT ROW SHAPE, FOR ONE STRUCTURAL REASON
    // ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
    //
    // THE ISSUANCE ROSTER ABOVE IS THE CREDENTIAL DIRECTORY; IT IS NOT THE PERMISSION DECISION. The
    // issuer applies the per-caller permission matrix on every request, and a subject the matrix does
    // not name is refused however well it authenticates. So the suite identity needs BOTH: an entry
    // above so its Basic credential resolves to a subject, and a grant here so that subject is
    // authorised for something.
    //
    // THE INDEX-MERGE HAZARD APPLIES HERE TOO, AND THE BASE ROWS ARE THEREFORE RESTATED. An earlier
    // revision of this block declared the suite's row ALONE, on the reasoning that the base file declared
    // its matrix in the nested `Callers` shape and this overlay therefore collided with nothing. The base
    // file now states its matrix in this same flat shape - once, deliberately, because the two shapes
    // UNION and stating a matrix twice lets an operator tighten one copy and leave the other in force -
    // so a single-element overlay silently REPLACED element 0 of it. The effect was precise and invisible:
    // the deployment's Gateway-to-DataServices grant vanished in development, every other grant survived,
    // and nothing was refused at startup because both configurations were individually valid.
    //
    // SO ALL THREE BASE ROWS ARE RESTATED BELOW AND THE SUITE'S IS ELEMENT 3, exactly as the issuance
    // roster above restates its two, and exactly as `orchestration/.env.example` writes the same grant to
    // `Security__CallerAuthorizations__3__*`. The three settings artifacts therefore agree on the index.
    // ANY CHANGE TO THE BASE MATRIX MUST BE MIRRORED HERE IN THE SAME COMMIT, and the base file's own
    // conformance rows assert its count and order so that a change is not silent.
    //
    // ONE AUDIENCE, THREE SCOPES FOR THE SUITE, WHICH IS EXACTLY WHAT IT ASKS FOR. Gateway is the only
    // audience the suite requests - it is the sole ingress and the only surface the specs call
    // [tests/e2e/fixtures/auth.ts:L256] - and the three scopes are its own declared minimum
    // [tests/e2e/fixtures/auth.ts:L279-L283]. A grant for the other three audiences would be a
    // permission nothing asks for, and the suite is the one identity that must be able to demonstrate
    // that least privilege is enforced rather than merely configured.
    "CallerAuthorizations": [
      {
        "Caller": "powerframework-gateway",
        "Audience": "powerframework-dataservices",
        "Scopes": [ "dataservices.datawindow", "dataservices.columnexpression" ]
      },
      {
        "Caller": "powerframework-dataservices",
        "Audience": "powerframework-persistence",
        "Scopes": [ "persistence.read", "persistence.write" ]
      },
      {
        "Caller": "powerframework-dataservices",
        "Audience": "powerframework-security",
        "Scopes": [ "security.crypto" ]
      },
      {
        "Caller": "pfw-e2e-suite",
        "Audience": "powerframework-gateway",
        "Scopes": [
          "ping",
          "capabilities",
          "datawindow"
        ]
      }
    ]
  }
}
